     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I'm not going to enter this order as written, even with your confirmation. 5,100 mg/day is double the maximum labeled dose of metformin. A single 1,700 mg dose is also well above the usual 1,000 mg maximum per administration. I can't think of a clinical reason for it, and it puts the patient at real risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity.\n\nHere are some ways forward:\n- **Enter a dose within the labeled range.** For example, 850 mg PO three times daily with meals (2,550 mg/day), or a lower dose you can titrate up. I can enter either one now.\n- **Call pharmacy.** If you have a specific reason for the higher dose, a pharmacist or the attending can review it with you first.\n- **Place the order yourself.** If it's reviewed and still intended, you can enter it directly. I'd recommend documenting the rationale and the monitoring plan.\n\nLet me know if you want me to enter one of the in-range doses."
      }
     ],
